                <h2>The Key Is a Single Reconciliation Standard, Not a Balance Calculation</h2>
<p>The clear conclusion is that overseas ad deposits, spend, and invoices should be maintained as separate records and then consolidated under consistent rules. Comparing only bank transactions with platform balances often produces misleading results.</p>
<p>An advertising account’s closing balance is not determined solely by deposits and campaign spend. Balance carryover, prepaid credits, promotional grants, taxes, agency fees, payment processing charges, refunds, and timing adjustments can all create discrepancies. Another common issue is the absence of a shared scope: finance may reconcile by calendar month, agencies may report by platform billing cycle, and performance teams may view spend according to ad delivery dates.</p>
<p>Maintain at least three detailed ledgers:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cash transaction ledger:</strong> Record every deposit, refund, promotional grant, and account transfer, including the payment transaction reference.</li>
<li><strong>Advertising spend ledger:</strong> Consolidate spend by account, currency, platform, and delivery date, and flag subsequent refunds or corrections.</li>
<li><strong>Invoice and fee ledger:</strong> Record platform invoices, agency rebates, service fees, taxes, and payment processing charges.</li>
</ul>
<p>These records should be linked through platform ID, advertising account ID, currency, and billing period so that every transaction remains traceable.</p>
<h2>Five Steps for Monthly Reconciliation</h2>
<h3>1. Define the reconciliation scope</h3>
<p>Confirm the legal entities, advertising platforms, accounts, currencies, and periods included in the reconciliation. Meta, Google Ads, TikTok Ads, and other platforms may use different billing cycles. Cross-time-zone campaigns should also be mapped to a defined billing date rather than simply grouped by local calendar days.</p>
<h3>2. Match deposits with confirmed platform credits</h3>
<p>Match bank or payment provider statements against platform transaction records, focusing on the amount, currency, processing fee, and status. A bank showing payment as completed does not necessarily mean the platform has credited the funds. Prepaid cards and corporate accounts may also display a successful payment while the deposit is still pending.</p>
<p>For deposits made through an agency, distinguish the actual amount paid, the amount credited by the platform, and the agency service fee. Combining these into a single deposit amount makes reconciliation unreliable.</p>
<h3>3. Verify the movement in account balances</h3>
<p>Use the following reconciliation logic:</p>
<p><code>Opening balance + deposits + grants - spend - fees - refunds + adjustments = closing balance</code></p>
<p>When a discrepancy appears, check balance carryover, unsettled spend, grant restrictions, refunds, taxes, and payment processing fees before drawing conclusions. Do not calculate spend by subtracting deposits from the closing balance, because this approach fails to account for timing differences and credit balances.</p>
<h3>4. Reconcile invoices against spend details</h3>
<p>Platform invoices generally correspond to a billing statement or a specific settlement period and may not equal the amount paid during the same calendar month. Use the platform’s spend detail and invoice line items instead of comparing invoice totals alone.</p>
<p>For example, one cross-border e-commerce team found that advertising spend broadly matched its payment amount, but finance had paid more than the platform had credited. The difference came from payment processing fees on a prepaid card and a separately paid agency service fee. Comparing only invoice totals would have incorrectly suggested that the platform had overcharged the team.</p>
<h3>5. Document evidence and classify discrepancies</h3>
<p>For each discrepancy, record the amount, currency, reason, owner, and resolution status. Supporting evidence should include platform statements, invoices, bank records, payment references, and agency settlement documents. A balance difference should not be included directly in advertising cost until it has been resolved.</p>
<h2>Discrepancies to Investigate First</h2>
<p><strong>The bank debit is recorded, but the platform has not credited the funds:</strong> Check whether the payment is pending, whether the currency matches, whether the correct legal entity paid, and whether a card authorization has been mistaken for a final charge.</p>
<p><strong>The platform balance is lower than expected:</strong> Review carryover balances, unsettled spend, expiring promotional grants, and delayed refunds.</p>
<p><strong>The invoice exceeds recorded spend:</strong> The difference may include taxes, platform fees, agency service fees, or items from another billing period. Confirm it through the invoice detail.</p>
<p><strong>Spend has declined, but the total payment has not changed:</strong> Check prepaid card balances and unsettled amounts before concluding that advertising performance has deteriorated.</p>
<p><strong>Amounts from different platforms cannot be added directly:</strong> Convert all currencies using a consistent rate source and rate date. Actual settlement exchange rates may be appropriate for financial close, while a standardized month-end rate may be used for management reporting. The two methods should not be mixed within the same cost table.</p>
<h2>A Reusable Monthly Close SOP</h2>
<p>At a fixed point each month, export platform spend, balances, invoices, and transaction records. Finance should provide bank and payment channel statements, while agencies submit rebate and service fee schedules. Match records account by account, resolve major discrepancies first, then review smaller processing fees, and obtain confirmation from performance, finance, and agency teams.</p>
<p>The objective is not merely to make every total appear equal. Every deposit should have a traceable destination, every amount of spend should be supported by an invoice or statement, and every variance should have a documented explanation. This level of control enables more accurate channel cost, budget availability, and advertising return analysis.</p>
